.TH NCLR 1 "August 2026" "nclr 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nclr \- NAND media erase / reinitialize CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nclr ls [\-j]
.br
.B nclr info [\-j] DEVICE
.br
.B nclr plan [\-l LEVEL] [OPTIONS] DEVICE
.br
.B nclr run [\-l LEVEL] [OPTIONS] DEVICE
.br
.B nclr run \-\-plan FILE|\- [OPTIONS]
.br
.B nclr check [\-j] [OPTIONS] DEVICE
.br
.B nclr salvage DEVICE \-\-output FILE \-\-map FILE [OPTIONS]
.br
.B nclr resume [OPTIONS] STATE
.br
.B nclr help [COMMAND]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nclr
erases and reinitializes one managed NAND medium (SD/microSD, USB flash) to
a reusable raw-uninitialized block device state. It is a one-shot, one-medium
CLI: no daemon, no API, no internal database.
.PP
When the backend has a documented device-level erase (SCSI SANITIZE BLOCK
ERASE, native SD full-range ERASE), the strongest plan is C2: the device
erase runs last, followed by a blank sweep, signature check, power cycle and
re-enumeration check. When a production controller profile matches the device
exactly, the C3 plan runs the controller reinitialization (BBT capture,
service mode, per-block old RBB erase, PROGRAM/READ/ECC qualification, new
BBT/spare/FTL rebuild with old FTL invalidation, capacity accounting). Real
Phison, Alcor and SMI execution additionally requires an authenticated exact
tuple protocol recipe and independent qualification report.
Otherwise the L1 recipe overwrites the full logical space with a
plan-seeded PRBS pattern, flushes, power cycles, verifies, overwrites with
zeros, verifies again, and checks that no partition/filesystem signatures
remain. Evidence (C grade, residual risk, H grade) is reported
independently; the final state is always
.B raw-uninitialized
and no filesystem is created.
.SH DANGER
Destructive. Verify the device path, fingerprint and capacity before running.
The run confirmation token is of the form
.BR DEVICE-FINGERPRINT8-CAPACITY ,
for example
.BR sdb-1d4a8c2e-31GB .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ls [\-j]
List removable whole-disk candidates, one device path per line (\-j: JSON).
.TP
.B info [\-j] DEVICE
Read-only identity, transport, capacity, fingerprint and backend capability.
.TP
.B plan [\-l LEVEL] [OPTIONS] DEVICE
Produce a normalized JSON plan on stdout (read-only).
.TP
.B run [\-l LEVEL] [OPTIONS] DEVICE
Generate a plan internally, confirm, and execute (destructive).
.TP
.B run \-\-plan FILE|\- [OPTIONS]
Execute a fixed plan file (or stdin). The plan hash, fingerprint, physical
path and capacity are validated before any destructive action.
.TP
.B check [\-j] [\-\-scratch\-range START:SECTORS] [\-\-yes] DEVICE
Non-destructive assessment: identity, sample reads, warnings. Never writes,
except for an explicit bounded write test: \-\-scratch\-range START:SECTORS
(with \-\-yes confirmation) writes a limited range and restores it, e.g.
\-\-scratch\-range 0:1024. Results are reported in the check JSON
(samples, vendor_health, scratch_test, errors).
.TP
.B salvage DEVICE \-\-output FILE \-\-map FILE [OPTIONS]
Enter the exactly matched controller service mode and read every declared
physical NAND page, including OOB bytes, without erase or program commands.
FILE is a new raw image in flat-block-major, page-minor, data-then-OOB order.
The mandatory NDJSON map records geometry, disposition, offsets, ECC/retry
metrics and unreadable zero-filled holes. Neither output may already exist.
.TP
.B resume [OPTIONS] STATE
Continue from an append-only journal state file after an interruption. The
device is re-identified and the backend queried before continuing.
.TP
.B help [COMMAND]
Show help for a subcommand.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-level LEVEL
Processing level: best (default), lba, device, controller, physical.
C1 (lba), C2 (device), C3 (controller) and C4 (physical) are implemented.
C2 requires a documented device-level erase capability; C3 requires a
production controller profile matching the device exactly; C4 requires a
certified physical-scope backend (independent validation, TEST-001).
Controller and physical plans never switch to a weaker erase method after
their first state-changing action; recovery/resume is required on failure.
.TP
.B \-\-min-level LEVEL
Minimum C grade the run result must meet (else degraded, exit 1).
.TP
.B \-\-no-fallback
Do not fall back to a weaker path (e.g. zero-only after PRBS write failure).
.TP
.B \-\-backend ID
Force a specific backend; rejected if it does not match the device.
.TP
.B \-\-backend-dir DIR
Additional backend search directory (repeatable; also NCLR_BACKEND_DIR).
.TP
.B \-\-artifact-dir DIR
Content-addressed controller artifact store for run/resume (repeatable; also
NCLR_ARTIFACT_DIR). Every plan-pinned object is opened without following
symlinks and revalidated by size, SHA-256 and format before confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-state FILE
Journal/state file location (default:
/var/lib/nclr/run/<plan-id>.state for root, $XDG_STATE_HOME/nclr otherwise).
Controller runs also create a two-slot, SHA-256 protected FILE.controller
state used for power-loss recovery.
.TP
.B \-\-events-fd N
Write NDJSON progress events to fd N (never mixed with stdout).
.TP
.B \-\-backend-timeout SECS
Kill a backend call that exceeds SECS (0 disables; default 0). The run
becomes interrupted (exit 75) and the journal records the boundary; nothing
is resent without a device status query (SAFE-003).
.TP
.B \-\-config FILE
Site policy file (default /etc/nclr/nclr.toml): allowed_backends,
minimum_level, spare_ratio bounds, power-cycle command allowlist.
.TP
.B \-\-unmount
Unmount target mounts (normal unmount only) before proceeding.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the interactive confirmation prompt; safety checks are never skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-power-cycle CMD
Approved external power control command, executed between verify phases.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-nonremovable
Allow non-removable targets (system disk protection remains absolute).
.TP
.B \-\-aggressive-lba
Research option: finite extra PRBS churn passes (grade ceiling stays C1).
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Emit the redacted summary report (nclr.summary.v1: grades, residual, final
state; identity fields are omitted) instead of the full report.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-json
Machine-readable JSON output (stdout carries only the JSON document).
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output; warnings and errors remain.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Increase diagnostic detail (repeatable).
.SH STREAMS
Data (final reports, plans, listings) goes to stdout. Diagnostics, progress
and confirmation prompts go to stderr. Machine progress events go to
\-\-events-fd as one JSON object per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Requested reach achieved and post-verification passed.
.TP
.B 1
Completed but degraded (residual risk, health H1, or min level unmet).
.TP
.B 2
No safe processing path; requested level cannot be planned.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.TP
.B 69
Required backend or external capability unavailable.
.TP
.B 74
Device I/O or protocol error.
.TP
.B 75
Interrupted; a resumable journal state was left.
.TP
.B 77
Permission or safety interlock rejection.
.TP
.B 78
Invalid profile, plan, signature or schema.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Plan and execute with a fixed plan:
.nf
nclr plan -l best /dev/sdb > card.plan.json
sudo nclr run --plan card.plan.json -j > card.report.json
.fi
.TP
One-shot interactive run:
.nf
sudo nclr run -l best /dev/sdb
.fi
.TP
Monitor progress with events:
.nf
sudo nclr run --plan card.plan.json --events-fd 9 9> events.ndjson
.fi
.TP
Read the complete physical NAND address space for salvage:
.nf
sudo nclr salvage /dev/sdb --output card.raw --map card.pages.ndjson
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
nclr-lab(1), nclr-backend(7).
